# A generic call whose body is its literal argument still has the type the call
# returns — `identity(300)` is an `i64` — so binding it as a `u8` is a
# narrowing, refused like any other. Only a literal the checker sees takes its
# type from the annotation.

import { print, to_str } from builtins;

fn identity<T: any>(x: T) -> T { x }

fn main() !prints {
    let r: u8 = identity(300);
    print(to_str(r));
}
--- errors ---
error: binding "r" is declared u8, but its value is i64 — u8 cannot hold every i64, so convert with `.narrow()`, which gives `none` when the value does not fit
  --> ./tests/cases/generics/errors/err_int_literal_arg_out_of_range.aipl:11:17
   |
11 |     let r: u8 = identity(300);
   |                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
